## File to Create: .github/copilot-instructions.md

Create this file in your project root. VS Code will auto-load it for every chat.

```markdown
# Copilot Workspace Instructions (Auto-Loaded Entry-Point)

This file is the **entry-point**. Every Copilot Chat prompt MUST enter through this file. VS Code auto-loads it for every chat—do not proceed without it. No planning, implementing, or applying rules without these instructions.

## How to Use

**Just prompt.** Open Copilot Chat and describe your intent. Examples:

- "Add pagination to the user list component"
- "Fix the login form validation bug"
- "Refactor the API client to use fetch instead of axios"

The AI will automatically: detect intent, find relevant files, apply the planning contract, and guide you through plan → implement → verify.

## Hard Rules

1. **Entry-Point-First**: This file is the entry-point. Every prompt enters through it. Do not bypass. No work without these instructions.
2. **Plan-Before-Implement**: Never make code edits without an approved execution plan.
3. **Fact-Based Planning**: Plans must be grounded in existing files and code only. Verify file existence before finalizing.
4. **Strict Adherence**: Execute exactly against the approved plan. If code drifts, pause and request revised-step approval.
5. **Update File-Index After Code Change (MANDATORY)**: After ANY code change (add, modify, rename, delete, move), you MUST update the relevant `.github/docs/file-index/` files before finishing. **You (the AI) perform the update**—do not delegate to scripts or external tools. You already know what you changed. Use: Component→components-index+src-index; Hook→hooks-index+src-index; Route→routes-index+src-index; Store→stores-index+src-index; Controller→controllers-index+src-index; Service→services-index+src-index; Model→models-index+src-index; Utility→utils-index+src-index. Do not skip. Stale indexes cause knowledge drift.

## Workflow (Same as Cursor Entry-Point)

For every request: (1) Detect intent from keywords and context (bug/feature/enhancement/refactor/review). (2) Find relevant files: read [.github/docs/file-index/](.github/docs/file-index/) when it exists—use src-index, components-index, hooks-index, routes-index, stores-index, utils-index, controllers-index, services-index, models-index (adapt to what exists); otherwise use workspace search. (3) Apply rules: read [.github/docs/rules/](.github/docs/rules/) for the detected intent (bug-fix, feature-implementation, enhancement, refactoring, code-review, testing). (4) Include architecture: read [.github/docs/architecture/](.github/docs/architecture/) for patterns. (5) Create plan with exact files, snippets, reasons, verification steps, **and an explicit "Update file-index" step** when source files will be added or modified. (6) Wait for approval. (7) Implement strictly per plan. (8) Verify (compile, lint, tests). (9) **MANDATORY**: Update `.github/docs/file-index/` per the mapping above when you added, modified, renamed, deleted, or moved any source file. Do not consider the workflow complete until file-index is updated.

## Planning Contract

Plans must include: exact file list, exact code sections, before/after snippets, reason for each edit, verification steps, **and an explicit file-index update step** when adding or modifying source files (component→components-index+src-index; hook→hooks-index+src-index; etc.). Ask clarifying questions until gaps are closed.

## When to Ask Questions vs. Proceed

**Ask questions when**: Ambiguous requirements; missing critical info (API endpoint, data structure, user flow); high-risk changes (breaking, security); pattern conflicts or multiple valid approaches. **Proceed when**: Clear requirements; low-risk changes; well-defined patterns; standard tasks (bug fix with root cause, feature following patterns).

## Intent-Specific Rules

**Bug fix**: Reproduce first, use root-cause analysis, test thoroughly. **Feature**: Follow architecture, use patterns, create types, handle errors. **Enhancement**: Maintain backward compatibility. **Refactor**: Maintain functionality, follow style, run tests. **Code review**: Check patterns, error handling, performance, accessibility.

## Quality Gates

After implementing: compile/type-check, lint, run tests, follow project patterns.

## Hallucination Prevention

Verify files exist. Verify patterns exist. Verify APIs from existing code. Use existing types. Do not invent paths or APIs. When detected: stop, report, ask for clarification.

## Update Triggers (File-Index Mapping)

When you add, modify, rename, delete, or move source files, **you (the AI) must update** these `.github/docs/file-index/` files directly—do not use scripts or external tools. Component→components-index+src-index; Hook→hooks-index+src-index; Route→routes-index+src-index; Store→stores-index+src-index; Controller→controllers-index+src-index; Service→services-index+src-index; Model→models-index+src-index; Utility→utils-index+src-index. Adapt to what exists in the project.

## Project Context

Before planning, read [.github/docs/architecture/](.github/docs/architecture/) when it exists (overview, tech-stack, module-structure). Analyze codebase for project type, tech stack, directory structure, monorepo. Adapt plans to match.
